1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,478
Your universe file is four hundred lines long.

2
00:00:04,378 --> 00:00:08,726
It works, and it's getting hard to find anything in.

3
00:00:10,526 --> 00:00:12,265
Today we tidy up.

4
00:00:13,165 --> 00:00:16,643
The jobs get a file of their own,

5
00:00:17,093 --> 00:00:19,702
and so does each side's dialogue.

6
00:00:21,502 --> 00:00:24,980
And then there's a reward for the housekeeping.

7
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:31,533
We print the whole world, as a book, a script, and a website.

8
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:35,776
Here's the whole idea, in four lines.

9
00:00:36,676 --> 00:00:38,850
A chapter heading, a fence,

10
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:42,778
and inside it, File, and a file's name.

11
00:00:44,578 --> 00:00:48,057
When the game starts, it reads that file,

12
00:00:48,507 --> 00:00:51,550
and puts its records into this chapter.

13
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:55,059
As far as the game's concerned,

14
00:00:55,509 --> 00:00:57,683
you typed them right here.

15
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:05,135
There are three rules, and I'll show you each one as we go.

16
00:01:06,335 --> 00:01:09,813
A new file, jobs dot a m d.

17
00:01:10,713 --> 00:01:16,800
I cut the six jobs out of the main file, and paste them in.

18
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,339
Now for rule one.

19
00:01:21,239 --> 00:01:25,152
In the main file, a job has three hashes,

20
00:01:25,602 --> 00:01:29,515
because it sits under a title and a chapter.

21
00:01:30,415 --> 00:01:34,763
In a chapter file there's no title and no chapter.

22
00:01:35,663 --> 00:01:39,141
So every record here starts with one hash.

23
00:01:40,941 --> 00:01:42,680
Replace All does it,

24
00:01:43,130 --> 00:01:48,783
and it tells me six changes, which is how many jobs I have.

25
00:01:50,583 --> 00:01:54,930
Back in the main file, the chapter gets its fence.

26
00:01:56,130 --> 00:01:59,609
Dialogue is the big one, with twenty records.

27
00:02:00,509 --> 00:02:03,117
I'm sorting them by who's speaking,

28
00:02:03,567 --> 00:02:07,046
one file for each side, in a folder.

29
00:02:08,846 --> 00:02:11,889
A captain's lines go with her side.

30
00:02:12,789 --> 00:02:15,398
That's a habit, not a rule.

31
00:02:17,198 --> 00:02:18,937
And here's rule two.

32
00:02:19,837 --> 00:02:22,446
Three files, one line, with commas.

33
00:02:24,246 --> 00:02:29,028
You'll also see a line for each file, one under another.

34
00:02:29,928 --> 00:02:31,667
That works as well,

35
00:02:32,117 --> 00:02:35,596
and the game reads every one of them.

36
00:02:36,796 --> 00:02:39,404
Now lint, and it's not clean.

37
00:02:40,304 --> 00:02:43,783
Three warnings, and they're all about one word.

38
00:02:45,583 --> 00:02:48,191
Remember the signal from Lecture Seven.

39
00:02:49,091 --> 00:02:53,874
An answer sends it, and a story beat waits for it.

40
00:02:54,774 --> 00:02:57,817
Those two are in different files now,

41
00:02:58,267 --> 00:03:02,615
and today, lint reads signals one file at a time.

42
00:03:04,415 --> 00:03:10,067
So it tells each file the other half is missing, and it isn't.

43
00:03:10,967 --> 00:03:15,315
The game reads everything into one universe before it runs.

44
00:03:17,115 --> 00:03:20,593
I'd rather show you this than hide it.

45
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:25,841
For this lecture, done means these three and no others.

46
00:03:27,041 --> 00:03:33,998
You might think, I'll leave the miners' call in the main file, next to the beat.

47
00:03:34,898 --> 00:03:37,941
That's rule three, and it doesn't help.

48
00:03:39,741 --> 00:03:43,654
A chapter reads from files, or it holds records.

49
00:03:44,554 --> 00:03:47,163
Do both, and the game's happy,

50
00:03:47,613 --> 00:03:51,091
but lint gets five different warnings, also wrong.

51
00:03:52,891 --> 00:03:55,935
So it's all in, or all out.

52
00:03:56,835 --> 00:03:59,878
If you really want a clean lint,

53
00:04:00,328 --> 00:04:04,676
leave the dialogue chapter whole and split only the jobs.

54
00:04:05,876 --> 00:04:08,485
One new thing for the crew.

55
00:04:09,385 --> 00:04:12,428
A captain is hailed at a station.

56
00:04:12,878 --> 00:04:16,357
A member of the cast doesn't need one.

57
00:04:18,157 --> 00:04:20,765
This is the relay's duty voice.

58
00:04:21,665 --> 00:04:24,274
It has a face, a color,

59
00:04:24,724 --> 00:04:29,507
and a scene, which is the record that speaks for it.

60
00:04:31,307 --> 00:04:35,654
The scene goes in a dialogue file, like any other.

61
00:04:36,554 --> 00:04:38,728
Notice there are no guards.

62
00:04:39,628 --> 00:04:41,802
The cast keeps no standing,

63
00:04:42,252 --> 00:04:48,339
so use them for what a crew should always be able to find out.

64
00:04:49,539 --> 00:04:54,322
And one more file, called exactly lore dot a m d.

65
00:04:56,122 --> 00:04:59,165
There are no fences in this one.

66
00:05:00,065 --> 00:05:02,239
A heading is a page,

67
00:05:02,689 --> 00:05:07,037
and the words under it are what the crew reads.

68
00:05:08,837 --> 00:05:11,446
The crew's handheld has a Library,

69
00:05:11,896 --> 00:05:15,374
and this file becomes a chapter in it.

70
00:05:17,174 --> 00:05:21,087
Put in what somebody who lives here would know.

71
00:05:21,987 --> 00:05:25,030
Keep the secrets of your story out.

72
00:05:26,230 --> 00:05:27,970
Now for the reward.

73
00:05:28,870 --> 00:05:32,348
s b s docs, the folder, a title,

74
00:05:32,798 --> 00:05:35,841
and I ask for all four editions.

75
00:05:37,641 --> 00:05:40,250
The prose one is the book.

76
00:05:41,150 --> 00:05:43,759
The catalog sorts everything by kind.

77
00:05:44,659 --> 00:05:47,267
The screenplay is only the conversations.

78
00:05:47,717 --> 00:05:51,630
Read one aloud. It's the best test there is.

79
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:56,039
And the bible is the plot.

80
00:05:56,939 --> 00:05:58,678
Look at this beat.

81
00:05:59,128 --> 00:06:03,476
It knows the call leads here, across the two files.

82
00:06:05,276 --> 00:06:07,015
Then there's the website.

83
00:06:07,915 --> 00:06:11,393
One page for each file, a search box,

84
00:06:11,843 --> 00:06:16,191
and every answer is a link to where it goes.

85
00:06:17,091 --> 00:06:21,874
Zip the folder and send it to whoever's writing with you.

86
00:06:23,074 --> 00:06:26,117
And the game is the same game.

87
00:06:27,017 --> 00:06:30,496
There's the same station, with the same buttons.

88
00:06:31,396 --> 00:06:36,613
The levy comes from one file now, and the jobs from another.

89
00:06:38,413 --> 00:06:41,891
Here's the Library, with my pages in it.

90
00:06:43,691 --> 00:06:46,300
So, a last word about lint.

91
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:49,374
After a split, it's noisier,

92
00:06:49,824 --> 00:06:54,607
and it misses a couple of things it used to catch.

93
00:06:55,507 --> 00:07:00,289
The list on the page is longer today for that reason.

94
00:07:01,489 --> 00:07:03,228
Now it's your turn.

95
00:07:04,128 --> 00:07:08,911
Split your universe, and account for every warning lint gives you.

96
00:07:09,811 --> 00:07:13,289
Write a voice anyone can ask for directions,

97
00:07:13,739 --> 00:07:15,913
and four pages of lore.

98
00:07:17,713 --> 00:07:22,061
Then print the screenplay, and read one conversation out loud.

99
00:07:22,961 --> 00:07:26,439
Change a line because of what you heard.

100
00:07:28,239 --> 00:07:31,717
Next time, the crew gets off the ship.
